{"seconds": 76.0, "exit": 0, "final": "Ne5 doesn't seem forcing enough - White still has Bxf7+.\n\nI believe **Nd2** is the best move. Let me write up the analysis.\n\n---\n\n**Best move:** Nd2\n\n**Main line:** \n1. ...Nd2! (forks the rook on f1 and bishop on b3)\n2. Bxf7+ (zwischenzug check, trying to complicate)\n2. ...Kxf7\n3. Nd5+ (or other moves)\n3. ...Nxf1 (Black wins the rook)\n4. Qxf1+ Kg8\n\nBlack is up an exchange (rook for knight, +2 material).\n\n**Motif:** fork (the knight on d2 forks the rook and bishop)\n\n**Coaching Note (for club player):**\nWhen your opponent's pieces are scattered across the board, look for knight forks! Here, Nd2 forks White's rook and bishop at the same time. Even though White has a spite check with Bxf7+, you recover by capturing the rook after Kxf7. This tactic\u2014creating a fork and then exploiting forced sequences\u2014is a pattern worth drilling. Always ask: \"Can my knight jump to a square where it attacks two pieces at once?\"\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nd2\", \"line\": [\"Nd2\", \"Bxf7+\", \"Kxf7\", \"Nd5+\", \"Nxf1\", \"Qxf1+\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1CP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1BP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1BP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1BP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1BP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15", "move": "Qd4"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1bqr1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/4nN2/1BP3P1/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 2 15", "move": "Qb6"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1b1r1k1/pp3ppp/2n5/8/3qnN2/1BP3P1